<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Circus Maximus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Circo Massimo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Circus Maximus was an ancient Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-racing stadium and mass entertainment venue in the valley between the Aventine and Palatine hills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The first and largest stadium of ancient Rome, it measured 621 m in length and 118 m in width and could hold over 150,000 spectators. It was Rome's largest venue for ludi, the public games of religious festivals, with races, beast-hunts and processions, and became the model for circuses throughout the empire. The last known beast-hunt there took place in 523 and the last known races in 549. The site is now a public park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/circus-maximus.jpg" alt="Circus Maximus" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rabax63 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CircusMaximusSO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other scholars hold that most martyrdoms took place elsewhere in Rome, notably the <b>Circus Maximus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Third Samnite War, Quintus Fabius Maximus Gurges dedicated the first Roman temple of Venus, to Venus Obsequens, near the Aventine Hill and the <b>Circus Maximus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-19">19 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fire broke out near the <b>Circus Maximus</b> and raged for days, gutting ten of Rome's fourteen districts.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-18">18 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The fire started in the merchant shops around Rome's chariot stadium, the <b>Circus Maximus</b>, and burned for six days before it was brought under control, then reignited for three more.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
